{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.finite_maximum_concentration","record":{"id":"scalar.finite_maximum_concentration","kind":"claim","label":"Concentration of the finite SK maximum","status":"unverified","source_id":"authored_long_comparisons_20261024","locator":"Long record §11.1.2, passage gibbs-comparison, Gaussian Lipschitz bound and concentration estimate","statement":"For n>=2, M_n=max_sigma H_n(sigma)/n is sqrt((n-1)/(2n^2))-Lipschitz in the independent standard Gaussian SK couplings. Gaussian concentration gives Pr(|M_n-E M_n|>a)<=2 exp(-n^2 a^2/(n-1)); E M_n tends to P_star, hence M_n tends to P_star in probability. No effective mean-bias rate is supplied. The concentration bound is for every a>0.","assumption_ids":["assumptions.qaoa_model_and_limits"],"citation_ids":["authored_r8_scalar_finite_maximum_concentration"],"evidence_ids":["ordinary_deductions_review_20261026"],"boundary":"Ordinary authored deduction checked against retained mathematical inputs; no new Lean declaration, elaborated dependency, cross-model transport, Comparator acceptance or independent proof certification is asserted."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.finite_maximum_concentration","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/2f85413164f06365ea0d825befee447650da71d54080d5dd65e78a708891c560","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"short","subgraph_ref":"sha256:cfbe950801fd334d8fd957243fa3b1f716ef609148c5f2ff97d44f9a6443d82c/papers/short.json/support/0.3.1/77f0cf29ab577b42d8caa5b2831ebfb1ca170d4b13c1ef6897cadc8e93c2a834","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/gibbs-comparison","anchor":"gibbs-comparison","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/gibbs-comparison","anchor":"gibbs-comparison","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n\n### 11.1.2 Finite-temperature values and exact Gibbs samples\n\nThis comparison samples the exact Gibbs distribution. The QAOA Born law\nin §11.2 is a separate distribution; none of the following bounds identifies\nthe two. For one fixed instance, let $L_n=\\max_\\sigma H_{n,J}(\\sigma)/n$,\n$\\mathcal F_{n,\\beta}=(n\\beta)^{-1}\\log\\sum_\\sigma e^{\\beta H_{n,J}(\\sigma)}$,\nand let $\\mathcal U_{n,\\beta}$ be the mean energy per spin under the\nGibbs law proportional to $e^{\\beta H_{n,J}(\\sigma)}$. Then\n$$\n0\\le\\mathcal F_{n,\\beta}-L_n\\le\\frac{\\log2}{\\beta},\\qquad\n0\\le L_n-\\mathcal U_{n,\\beta}\\le\\frac{\\log2}{\\beta}.\n$$\nThe first is the log-sum bound. The second uses\n$\\mathcal F=\\mathcal U+S/(n\\beta)$, where the Gibbs entropy satisfies\n$0\\le S\\le n\\log2$, together with $\\mathcal U\\le L_n\\le\\mathcal F$.\nThus both disorder averages approach $P_*$ along every joint\n$n\\to\\infty$, $\\beta\\to\\infty$, by the already established\nconvergence of $\\mathbb E L_n$.\n\nThere is also a finite probability estimate. Every configuration has\nGaussian coefficient-vector norm $\\sqrt{(n-1)/(2n^2)}$ after division\nby $n$, and their maximum has the same Lipschitz bound. Gaussian\nconcentration gives, for $n\\ge2$,\n$$\n\\Pr_J(|L_n-\\mathbb E L_n|>s)\n\\le2\\exp\\{-n^2s^2/(n-1)\\}.\n$$\nFor an exact Gibbs draw $\\Sigma^{\\rm G}$, count the at most $2^n$\nconfigurations with deficit at least $s$ and bound the partition sum\nbelow by its maximal term. This gives, for every instance,\n$$\n\\Pr\\{H_{n,J}(\\Sigma^{\\rm G})/n\\le L_n-s\\mid J\\}\n\\le\\min\\{1,e^{n(\\log2-\\beta s)}\\}.\n$$\nThe lower tail below $P_*-s$ is bounded by\n$\\Pr_J(L_n<P_*-s/2)+\\min\\{1,e^{n(\\log2-\\beta s/2)}\\}$;\nthe upper tail is at most the upper tail of $L_n$. Hence an exact\nGibbs sample also approaches $P_*$ in joint probability. These are\nelementary scalar consequences of the Gibbs law, entropy and Gaussian\nconcentration. They supply neither an efficient sampler nor QAOA angles.\n","span":{"start_byte":219370,"end_byte":221279,"start_line":4740,"end_line":4781},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=gibbs-comparison","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=gibbs-comparison"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=scalar.finite_maximum_concentration&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=scalar.finite_maximum_concentration&direction=consumers&assumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=scalar.finite_maximum_concentration&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=scalar.finite_maximum_concentration&relation=prerequisites"},"relations":[{"family":"arguments","id":"field:235916b25ed73b9be67e5